(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-12-02"] = {"mth-12-02-m01":{"idea":"There are three elementary row operations, and each can be undone.","steps":["Swap two rows.","Multiply a row by a non-zero number.","Add a multiple of one row to another row.","Multiplying a row by zero would destroy the row, and it cannot be reversed, so it is not allowed."],"others":"Adding a multiple of a row, swapping rows and multiplying by a non-zero number are the three allowed operations.","trap":"The multiplier must be non-zero.","answer":"multiplying a row by the number zero"},"mth-12-02-m02":{"idea":"For a 2 × 2 matrix, the inverse is the adjoint divided by the determinant.","steps":["|A| = 2 × 3 − 1 × 5 = 1.","Swap the diagonal entries and change the signs of the other two: adj A = [[3, −1], [−5, 2]].","A⁻¹ = adj A/|A| = [[3, −1], [−5, 2]]."],"others":"[[2, −1], [−5, 3]] does not swap the diagonal. [[3, 1], [5, 2]] forgets the signs. [[−3, 1], [5, −2]] has all signs reversed.","trap":"Swap the diagonal, flip the signs of the other two.","answer":"[[3, −1], [−5, 2]]"},"mth-12-02-m03":{"idea":"A matrix has no inverse when its determinant is zero.","steps":["For [[1, 2], [2, 4]]: 1 × 4 − 2 × 2 = 0.","So the matrix is singular.","Its second row is twice the first row."],"others":"The other three have determinants −2, 6 and 1, which are not zero, so they all have inverses.","trap":"Zero determinant means no inverse.","answer":"[[1, 2], [2, 4]], as its determinant is 0"},"mth-12-02-m04":{"idea":"The inverse of a product reverses the order.","steps":["Check (AB)(B⁻¹A⁻¹) = A(BB⁻¹)A⁻¹ = AIA⁻¹ = AA⁻¹ = I.","So B⁻¹A⁻¹ is the inverse of AB.","The order is reversed, like the transpose of a product."],"others":"the inverse of a sum and the sum of the inverses do not work for sums. A⁻¹B⁻¹ keeps the wrong order.","trap":"Shoes and socks: the last on is the first off.","answer":"B⁻¹A⁻¹, with the order reversed"},"mth-12-02-m05":{"idea":"A matrix times its adjoint gives the determinant times the identity.","steps":["A·adj(A) = adj(A)·A = |A| I.","If |A| is not zero, A⁻¹ = adj A/|A|.","If |A| = 0, the product is the zero matrix."],"others":"It equals I only when |A| = 1. |A| A is not the result. The last option is a number, not a matrix.","trap":"The answer is a matrix: |A| times I.","answer":"|A| I, the determinant times the identity matrix"},"mth-12-02-m06":{"idea":"For an n × n matrix, |adj A| = |A| raised to n − 1.","steps":["Take determinants of A·adj(A) = |A| I.","For n = 3: |A| × |adj A| = |A|³.","So |adj A| = |A|² = 4² = 16."],"others":"8 uses |A| × 2. 4 uses |A|¹. 64 uses |A|³.","trap":"Power is n − 1, not n.","answer":"16"},"mth-12-02-m07":{"idea":"For a 2 × 2 matrix [[a, b], [c, d]] the adjoint is [[d, −b], [−c, a]].","steps":["Here a = 3, b = 4, c = 5, d = 6.","Swap the diagonal entries: 6 and 3.","Change the signs of the other two: −4 and −5.","adj A = [[6, −4], [−5, 3]]."],"others":"[[3, −4], [−5, 6]] does not swap the diagonal. [[6, 4], [5, 3]] misses the sign changes. [[−6, 4], [5, −3]] changes the wrong signs.","trap":"Swap the diagonal, negate the off-diagonal.","answer":"[[6, −4], [−5, 3]]"},"mth-12-02-m08":{"idea":"The determinant of the inverse is the reciprocal of the determinant.","steps":["A A⁻¹ = I.","Take determinants: |A| |A⁻¹| = 1.","|A⁻¹| = 1/|A| = 1/5."],"others":"1/25 squares the reciprocal. 5 ignores the inverse. −1/5 changes the sign without reason.","trap":"Reciprocal, with the same sign.","answer":"1/5"},"mth-12-02-m09":{"idea":"Swapping two rows changes the sign of the determinant.","steps":["Interchange two rows.","The determinant changes its sign.","Its absolute value stays the same."],"others":"It does not become zero unless the rows were equal. It does change sign. It is not doubled.","trap":"Track the sign when row reducing.","answer":"changes its sign, but keeps the same absolute value"},"mth-12-02-m10":{"idea":"Adding a multiple of one row to another does not change the determinant.","steps":["The operation is Rᵢ → Rᵢ + k Rⱼ.","The determinant stays the same.","That is why it is the main step in reducing to triangular form."],"others":"It does not change the sign. It does not multiply by the multiple. It does not make the determinant zero.","trap":"Swap changes sign. Add-a-multiple changes nothing.","answer":"stays the same"},"mth-12-02-m11":{"idea":"A common factor of one row can be taken out of the determinant.","steps":["Multiplying one row by k multiplies the determinant by k.","Here k = 5.","So the determinant is multiplied by 5."],"others":"It does change. 15 and 125 would need all three rows multiplied (5³ = 125).","trap":"One row: factor k. All rows of a 3 × 3: factor k³.","answer":"is multiplied by 5"},"mth-12-02-m12":{"idea":"If the determinant of the coefficient matrix is not zero, the inverse exists and gives the solution.","steps":["If |A| ≠ 0, then A⁻¹ exists.","Multiply AX = B on the left by A⁻¹.","X = A⁻¹B, and it is unique."],"others":"X = BA⁻¹ has the wrong order. A zero B does not decide uniqueness. |A| = 0 means no inverse.","trap":"Matrix multiplication order matters.","answer":"|A| is not zero, and the solution is X = A⁻¹B"},"mth-12-02-m13":{"idea":"A homogeneous system has a non-trivial solution exactly when the determinant is zero.","steps":["If |A| ≠ 0, then X = A⁻¹O = O is the only solution, the trivial one.","If |A| = 0, the rows are dependent.","Then there are infinitely many solutions."],"others":"|A| not zero gives only the trivial solution. The identity matrix gives only the trivial solution. Having no zero element is not the condition.","trap":"Non-trivial needs |A| = 0.","answer":"|A| = 0"},"mth-12-02-m14":{"idea":"Parallel equations with different right sides have no solution.","steps":["The coefficient determinant is 1 × 2 − 1 × 2 = 0.","The second equation gives x + y = 5/2.","That contradicts x + y = 2, so the system is inconsistent."],"others":"Infinitely many solutions needs the right sides in the same ratio. A zero determinant alone does not mean inconsistency, so the second option is incomplete. One solution would need a non-zero determinant.","trap":"Zero determinant: check whether the right sides agree.","answer":"inconsistent, as the left sides are in proportion but the right sides are not"},"mth-12-02-m15":{"idea":"If the second equation is just a multiple of the first, there are infinitely many solutions.","steps":["The determinant of the coefficients is zero.","The second equation, 2x + 2y = 4, is twice the first.","So every pair with x + y = 2 satisfies both."],"others":"No solution would need conflicting right sides. Exactly one or two solutions do not occur for a linear system like this.","trap":"Zero determinant plus agreement: infinitely many.","answer":"infinitely many solutions, as the second equation is twice the first"},"mth-12-02-m16":{"idea":"The cofactor is the minor with a sign (−1)^(i + j).","steps":["Cofactor A_ij = (−1)^(i + j) M_ij.","For row 2, column 1: i + j = 3, so (−1)³ = −1.","So the cofactor is −1 times the minor."],"others":"The minor of another element is wrong. +1 times the minor applies when i + j is even. The product of the element and its minor is used in the expansion, but it is not the cofactor.","trap":"Checkerboard of signs: + − + / − + − / + − +.","answer":"−1 times the minor of that element"},"mth-12-02-m17":{"idea":"The inverse of an inverse is the matrix itself.","steps":["A⁻¹ A = A A⁻¹ = I.","That is exactly the condition for A to be the inverse of A⁻¹.","So (A⁻¹)⁻¹ = A."],"others":"adj A, −A and the transpose do not satisfy the inverse condition.","trap":"Like the reciprocal of a reciprocal.","answer":"A itself"},"mth-12-02-m18":{"idea":"To invert a scalar multiple, invert the scalar and the matrix.","steps":["Try (1/3)A⁻¹.","(3A)((1/3)A⁻¹) = (3 × 1/3) A A⁻¹ = I.","So (3A)⁻¹ = (1/3) A⁻¹."],"others":"−3A⁻¹ and 3A⁻¹ do not give I when multiplied. (1/3)A is not an inverse.","trap":"(kA)⁻¹ = (1/k) A⁻¹.","answer":"(1/3) A⁻¹"},"mth-12-02-m19":{"idea":"The inverse of a diagonal matrix is diagonal with the reciprocals.","steps":["A diagonal matrix with no zero on the diagonal has an inverse.","The inverse is diagonal with 1/2, 1/4 and 1/5.","The product of the two is the identity."],"others":"Negatives do not give the identity. 1/10 on each place is not the reciprocal of each. The same matrix does not give the identity.","trap":"Reciprocal of each diagonal entry.","answer":"the diagonal matrix with entries 1/2, 1/4 and 1/5"},"mth-12-02-m20":{"idea":"A matrix can have only one inverse.","steps":["Suppose B and C are both inverses of A.","Then B = BI = B(AC) = (BA)C = IC = C.","So there is only one inverse."],"others":"Many inverses is wrong by this argument. An inverse needs a square matrix. The inverse is not the transpose in general.","trap":"Inverse is unique.","answer":"it is unique, and only one matrix can satisfy AB = BA = I"},"mth-12-02-m21":{"idea":"Row operations that turn A into I turn I into A⁻¹.","steps":["The row operations are equal to multiplying by a matrix P.","PA = I, so P = A⁻¹.","The same operations turn I into PI = A⁻¹."],"others":"The transpose, the adjoint and A itself are not what the right side becomes.","trap":"[A | I] becomes [I | A⁻¹].","answer":"A⁻¹"},"mth-12-02-m22":{"idea":"If A is invertible, it can be cancelled on the left.","steps":["AB = AC.","Multiply on the left by A⁻¹: A⁻¹AB = A⁻¹AC.","So IB = IC and B = C."],"others":"B = C does not need A to be the identity. 'Nothing can be said' ignores the inverse. B = −C has no basis.","trap":"This needs |A| ≠ 0.","answer":"B = C, since A⁻¹ can be multiplied on the left"},"mth-12-02-m23":{"idea":"Scaling a 3 × 3 matrix by k multiplies its determinant by k³.","steps":["Each of the three rows has a common factor 2.","|2A| = 2³ |A| = 8 × 3 = 24."],"others":"6 and 18 use 2 or 2 × 3 × 3. 12 uses 2² × 3.","trap":"The factor is raised to the order of the matrix.","answer":"24"},"mth-12-02-m24":{"idea":"Solve the system using X = A⁻¹B.","steps":["Coefficient matrix [[2, 1], [1, 1]] has |A| = 2 − 1 = 1.","A⁻¹ = [[1, −1], [−1, 2]].","X = A⁻¹B = [[1 × 5 − 1 × 3], [−1 × 5 + 2 × 3]] = [2, 1].","So x = 2 and y = 1."],"others":"3, 5 and 1 do not satisfy both equations together.","trap":"Check: 2(2) + 1 = 5 and 2 + 1 = 3.","answer":"2"},"mth-12-02-n01":{"idea":"Expand the determinant along the first row.","steps":["|A| = 2(4 × 6 − 1 × 2) − 1(0 × 6 − 1 × 5) + 3(0 × 2 − 4 × 5).","= 2(22) − 1(−5) + 3(−20).","= 44 + 5 − 60 = −11."],"trap":"Watch the alternating signs + − +.","answer":"-11"},"mth-12-02-n02":{"idea":"An entry of the inverse is a cofactor divided by the determinant.","steps":["|A| = 1(0 − 24) − 2(0 − 20) + 3(0 − 5) = −24 + 40 − 15 = 1.","The (1, 1) entry of adj A is the cofactor of the (1, 1) entry: +(1 × 0 − 4 × 6) = −24.","The entry of A⁻¹ is −24/1 = −24."],"trap":"For the inverse, the entry (1, 1) uses the cofactor of (1, 1).","answer":"-24"},"mth-12-02-n03":{"idea":"Solve the 3 × 3 system, then compute x + 2y + 3z.","steps":["|A| = 1(1 − 2) − 1(−2 − 1) + 1(4 + 1) = −1 + 3 + 5 = 7, not zero.","Solving gives x = 1, y = 2, z = 3.","Then x + 2y + 3z = 1 + 4 + 9 = 14."],"trap":"Check: x + y + z = 6, 2 − 2 + 3 = 3, 1 + 4 − 3 = 2.","answer":"14"},"mth-12-02-n04":{"idea":"For a 3 × 3 matrix, |adj A| = |A|².","steps":["|A| = −11.","|adj A| = |A|² = (−11)² = 121."],"trap":"The square is always positive.","answer":"121"},"mth-12-02-n05":{"idea":"Set up the system with the prices as unknowns, then solve.","steps":["Let the prices be x, y and z for pen, notebook and ruler.","2x + 3y + z = 41, 3x + y + 2z = 37, x + 2y + 3z = 42.","Solving with the inverse gives x = 5, y = 8 and z = 7.","So a notebook costs ₹8."],"trap":"Check the first equation: 10 + 24 + 7 = 41.","answer":"8"},"mth-12-02-n06":{"idea":"Use A⁻¹ = adj A/|A|.","steps":["|A| = 4 × 6 − 7 × 2 = 24 − 14 = 10.","adj A = [[6, −7], [−2, 4]].","The (1, 2) element of A⁻¹ is −7/10 = −0.7."],"trap":"Keep the minus sign.","answer":"-0.7"},"mth-12-02-n07":{"idea":"A matrix is singular when its determinant is zero.","steps":["|A| = 1(4 − 5) − 2(3 − 10) + k(3 − 8).","= −1 + 14 − 5k = 13 − 5k.","This is zero for k = 13/5 = 2.6."],"trap":"Expand carefully along the first row.","answer":"2.6"},"mth-12-02-n08":{"idea":"The determinant of a product is the product of the determinants.","steps":["B is upper triangular, so |B| = 1 × 1 × 3 = 3.","|AB| = |A||B| = (−11) × 3 = −33."],"trap":"A triangular matrix's determinant is the product of its diagonal.","answer":"-33"},"mth-12-02-n09":{"idea":"Find the inverse, then add its four entries.","steps":["|A| = 2 × 4 − 3 × 1 = 5.","adj A = [[4, −3], [−1, 2]], so A⁻¹ = (1/5)[[4, −3], [−1, 2]].","The entries are 0.8, −0.6, −0.2 and 0.4.","Their sum is 0.4."],"trap":"Divide every entry of the adjoint by 5.","answer":"0.4"}};
